Chapter 3: The Cognitive Scale $\Sigma = (\tau, \lambda, \epsilon, \mu)$

3.1 Four Dimensions of Cognitive Architecture

We define a cognitive scale as a four-dimensional profile:

\Sigma = (\tau, \lambda, \epsilon, \mu)

where:

· $\tau$: Effective Temporal Horizon — The number of steps an agent can accurately simulate into the future. This is not the discount factor $\gamma$, which determines the agent's patience, but the planning depth over which the agent can maintain predictive accuracy. Formally, $\tau \in [1, \tau_{\max}] \subset \mathbb{R}_{++}$.
· $\lambda$: Spatial Reach — The maximum distance over which the agent can observe, act, or influence. In a grid world, this is a radius; at cosmic scales, it corresponds to the reach of telescopes, probes, or energy projection. Formally, $\lambda \in [0, \lambda_{\max}] \subset \mathbb{R}_{+}$.
· $\epsilon$: Exploration Propensity — The weight given to novel state visitation in the agent's objective function. High $\epsilon$ agents are more likely to venture into unknown territories; low $\epsilon$ agents prefer familiar domains. Formally, $\epsilon \in [0, 1]$.
· $\mu$: Modeling Capacity — The dimensionality of the latent state representation in the agent's world model. Higher $\mu$ enables more accurate predictions of complex environments and other agents' behaviors. Formally, $\mu \in [1, \mu_{\max}] \subset \mathbb{R}_{++}$.

3.2 Generalization of Existing Scales

This profile generalizes the Kardashev scale, which classifies civilizations by energy use (Type I: planetary, Type II: stellar, Type III: galactic), to four dimensions. It also generalizes Axelrod's shadow-of-the-future mechanism, which treats a single discount factor as the sole determinant of future weighting, to a multidimensional profile in which different capacities interact non-trivially.

The key insight is that $\tau$ and $\lambda$ are not merely "more" or "less" of the same thing. An agent with $\tau = 100, \lambda = 1$ is a patient local planner; an agent with $\tau = 1, \lambda = 100$ is an impulsive explorer. Their strategic behaviors may differ dramatically even though both are "high-capacity" in some intuitive sense.

3.3 The Horizon–Reach Interaction

The interaction between temporal horizon and spatial reach is particularly important. An agent can only exploit distant resources if both $\tau$ and $\lambda$ are sufficiently large:

\text{Effective exploration range} = \min(\lambda, \tau \cdot v_{\text{travel}})

where $v_{\text{travel}}$ is the agent's maximum travel velocity. This captures the intuitive point that seeing a distant resource is insufficient; the agent must also be able to plan the journey and survive the transit time.

