Part II: The Formal Model

Chapter 4: The Central Hypothesis in Conditional Form

4.1 The Abundant-Shareable Condition

Let $\mathcal{E} = (\mathcal{R}, \mathcal{S}, \nu, \mathcal{K})$ denote the environment, where $\mathcal{R}$ is resource abundance ratio, $\mathcal{S}$ is shareability index, $\nu$ is encounter frequency, and $\mathcal{K}$ is reversibility. Define the abundant-shareable condition:

\mathcal{E}_{\text{abundant}} \equiv \left\{ \mathcal{R} > \mathcal{R}_c, \ \mathcal{S} > \mathcal{S}_c, \ \text{and utility functions are saturating in resource consumption} \right\}

The thresholds $\mathcal{R}_c$ and $\mathcal{S}_c$ are operational proxies; the formal condition that does the real theoretical work is $\Delta R_{\text{flow}} > 2p_a D$ (Chapter 6).

4.2 The Primary Hypothesis

Our primary hypothesis is:

\mathbf{H1}_\tau: \quad \frac{\partial C_{\text{dom}}}{\partial \tau} < 0 \quad \text{under } \mathcal{E}_{\text{abundant}}

where $C_{\text{dom}}$ is the equilibrium proportion of actions allocated to domination. The hypothesis is deliberately conditional: under scarcity or excludability, the effect is predicted to reverse.

4.3 The Secondary Hypothesis

\mathbf{H2}_\tau: \quad \frac{\partial C_{\text{dom}}}{\partial \tau} > 0 \quad \text{under } \mathcal{E}_{\text{scarce}}

This reversal is not a weakness of the framework but its central prediction. Cognitive horizon is not inherently peaceful or aggressive; it is a magnifier of environmental structure.

