Chapter 5: The Opportunity-Cost Model with Dynamic Security Liability

5.1 Strategic Action Space

An agent $S_i$ with cognitive profile $\Sigma_i$ evaluates three strategic actions:

· Local Domination $A_{\text{dom}}$: Neutralizing adjacent agents through preemptive attack or resource exclusion.
· Distant Exploration $A_{\text{expl}}$: Expanding into unexplored territory in search of new resources.
· Cooperative Alignment $A_{\text{coop}}$: Negotiating non-aggression, resource sharing, or joint ventures with adjacent agents.

5.2 Threat Model and Security Liability

If agent $S_{-i}$ remains unneutralized in the local domain, it presents a persistent tail-risk. In each period, $S_{-i}$ executes an opportunistic attack against $S_i$'s origin assets with probability $p_a$. The dynamic threat liability over horizon $\tau_{\text{eff}}$ is:

\Omega_{\text{threat}}(\tau_{\text{eff}}) = \sum_{t=1}^{\infty} \gamma^t p_a D = (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1) p_a D

where $D$ is catastrophic asset loss upon attack and $\tau_{\text{eff}} = 1/(1-\gamma)$ is the effective horizon. The security premium of neutralizing $S_{-i}$ is therefore:

\Psi_{\text{security}}(S_{-i}; \tau_{\text{eff}}) = (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1) p_a D

This term captures the tail-risk neutralization incentive central to Bostrom's instrumental convergence argument. A long-horizon agent perceives a much greater threat from leaving a local adversary unneutralized than a short-horizon agent does.

5.3 Payoff Formulations

The three strategy payoffs are:

V(A_{\text{dom}}) = R_{\text{local}} - C_{\text{conflict}}(\lambda, \mu) + (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1) \left( R_{\text{local, flow}} + p_a D \right)

V(A_{\text{expl}}) = -C_{\text{travel}}(\lambda) + (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1) \left( \mathbb{E}[R_{\text{distant}}(\lambda, \epsilon, \mathcal{R})] - p_a D \right)

V(A_{\text{coop}}) = -C_{\text{diplomacy}}(\mu) + (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1) \left( R_{\text{shared}}(\mathcal{S}) - \delta(\mu) p_a D \right)

where:

· $R_{\text{local}}$: one-time resource gain from domination.
· $R_{\text{local, flow}}$: per-period resource flow from controlling local domain.
· $C_{\text{conflict}}(\lambda, \mu)$: one-time cost of conducting domination.
· $C_{\text{travel}}(\lambda)$: one-time cost of initiating exploration.
· $C_{\text{diplomacy}}(\mu)$: one-time cost of establishing cooperation.
· $\mathbb{E}[R_{\text{distant}}]$: expected per-period resource yield from distant domain.
· $R_{\text{shared}}(\mathcal{S})$: per-period resource yield from shared local domain.
· $\delta(\mu) \in [0,1)$: residual attack probability multiplier after diplomacy, with $\delta'(\mu) < 0$.

5.4 Assumptions and Their Justification

Assumption 5.1 (Time-Separable Payoffs): The payoffs are linear in the horizon factor $(\tau_{\text{eff}}-1)$. This assumes constant per-period flows and costs. In reality, resource yields may vary over time, and security threats may fluctuate.

Justification: This is a first-order approximation. The linearity assumption enables closed-form derivation of the critical horizon. Nonlinear extensions are discussed in §22.2.

Assumption 5.2 (Stationary Attack Probability): The baseline model treats $p_a$ as a stationary scalar. The endogenous formulation in Chapter 7 relaxes this assumption.

Justification: Stationary $p_a$ captures the average threat level. Endogenous $p_a$ captures strategic feedback but complicates the equilibrium analysis.

