Chapter 6: Derivation of the Critical Temporal Horizon

6.1 The Payoff Differential

The strategic payoff advantage of exploration over domination is:

\Delta V_{\text{expl} - \text{dom}} = -C_{\text{travel}} + (\tau_{\text{eff}}-1)\left(\Delta R_{\text{flow}} - 2p_a D\right) - R_{\text{local}} + C_{\text{conflict}}

where $\Delta R_{\text{flow}} \equiv \mathbb{E}[R_{\text{distant}}] - R_{\text{local, flow}}$ is the marginal distant yield advantage.

6.2 The Governing Derivative

Differentiating with respect to temporal horizon yields:

\frac{\partial}{\partial \tau_{\text{eff}}} \left[ V(A_{\text{expl}}) - V(A_{\text{dom}}) \right] = \Delta R_{\text{flow}}(\lambda, \epsilon, \mathcal{R}) - 2 p_a D

This derivative establishes the mathematical boundary conditions governing the hypothesis. Under environmental abundance where $\Delta R_{\text{flow}} > 2 p_a D$, increasing temporal horizon strictly decreases the relative utility of domination.

6.3 The Critical Temporal Horizon

The critical temporal horizon threshold at which exploration strictly suppresses local conflict is:

\tau_c = 1 + \frac{R_{\text{local}} + C_{\text{travel}}(\lambda) - C_{\text{conflict}}(\lambda, \mu)}{\Delta R_{\text{flow}}(\lambda, \epsilon, \mathcal{R}) - 2 p_a D}

This is a falsifiable prediction. If $\tau_c$ exists and is finite under $\mathcal{E}_{\text{abundant}}$, the hypothesis is supported. If no finite $\tau_c$ exists—that is, domination remains optimal for all horizons—the hypothesis is rejected.

6.4 Theorem 6.1: Horizon-Conflict Sign Reversal

Theorem (Horizon-Conflict Sign Reversal). Let $\Delta V(\tau_{\text{eff}}) = V(A_{\text{expl}}) - V(A_{\text{dom}})$. Then:

\frac{d}{d\tau_{\text{eff}}} \Delta V(\tau_{\text{eff}}) = \Delta R_{\text{flow}} - 2p_a D

Consequently:

1. If $\Delta R_{\text{flow}} > 2p_a D$, then $\Delta V$ is strictly increasing in $\tau_{\text{eff}}$, and domination propensity decreases with horizon.
2. If $\Delta R_{\text{flow}} < 2p_a D$, then $\Delta V$ is strictly decreasing in $\tau_{\text{eff}}$, and domination propensity increases with horizon.

Proof. Differentiate the explicit expression for $\Delta V$ with respect to $\tau_{\text{eff}}$. The derivative is the coefficient $\Delta R_{\text{flow}} - 2p_a D$, whose sign determines monotonicity. Since $C_{\text{dom}}$ is monotonically related to the relative value of $A_{\text{dom}}$ versus $A_{\text{expl}}$, the sign of $\partial C_{\text{dom}}/\partial \tau$ follows immediately. $\blacksquare$

6.5 Interpretation: The Role of $2p_a D$

The factor $2$ in $2p_a D$ arises because both strategies incur security costs in the baseline model: domination pays the one-time conflict cost but eliminates the threat, while exploration pays the travel cost and continues to face the threat. The term $p_a D$ therefore appears twice in the differential: once as the avoided loss under domination and once as the continuing liability under exploration.

When $\Delta R_{\text{flow}} > 2p_a D$, the distant yield advantage exceeds the combined security benefit of neutralization and the ongoing threat cost of exploration. Long horizons then favor exploration.

When $\Delta R_{\text{flow}} < 2p_a D$, the security benefit of neutralization dominates. Long horizons then favor preemptive domination.

