Chapter 7: Endogenous Attack Probability and Dynamic Threat Coupling

7.1 From Stationary to Endogenous Threat

The baseline model treats attack probability $p_a$ as a stationary scalar. In realistic multi-agent settings, $p_a$ is an endogenous variable that depends on the adversary's cognitive profile and the strategic context. We derive a closed-form endogenous attack probability.

7.2 Adversarial Payoff Differential

At time $t$, adversary $S_{-i}$ evaluates the net expected benefit of initiating a preemptive strike versus maintaining coexistence:

\Delta V_{-i}(t) = \mathbb{E}_{\mu_{-i}} \left[ \sum_{k=1}^{\tau_{-i}(t)} \gamma^k \left( \hat{R}_{-i}^{\text{attack}}(t+k) - \hat{R}_{-i}^{\text{coexist}}(t+k) \right) \right] - C_{\text{attack}}(\lambda_{-i}(t))

7.3 Epistemic Uncertainty

The adversary's estimate of $\Delta V_{-i}(t)$ contains noise due to incomplete information. The variance of this estimation noise decreases monotonically with adversary modeling capacity:

\sigma^2(\mu_{-i}) = \frac{\sigma_0^2}{1 + \alpha \mu_{-i}}

where $\sigma_0^2$ is the baseline uncertainty and $\alpha > 0$ is the modeling efficiency parameter.

7.4 Closed-Form Attack Probability

Assuming logistic estimation error parameterized by adversary risk tolerance $\epsilon_{-i}$:

p_a(t, \Sigma_{-i}) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp\left( - \frac{\Delta V_{-i}(t, \tau_{-i}, \lambda_{-i}) - \Theta_{\text{def}}}{\epsilon_{-i} \sqrt{\frac{\sigma_0^2}{1 + \alpha \mu_{-i}}}} \right)}

where $\Theta_{\text{def}}$ represents the defender's structural defense threshold.

7.5 Limiting Behavior

· Rational Omniscience ($\epsilon_{-i} \to 0, \mu_{-i} \to \infty$): $p_a$ collapses to a deterministic Heaviside step function: attack if $\Delta V_{-i} > \Theta_{\text{def}}$, refrain otherwise.
· Aggression Limit ($\epsilon_{-i} \to \infty$): $p_a \to 0.5$, representing random uncoordinated aggression.
· High Modeling Capacity ($\mu_{-i} \to \infty$): $\sigma(\mu_{-i}) \to 0$, making the logistic function steeper and attack decisions more decisive.

7.6 Dynamic Coupling

The cognitive profile components evolve dynamically:

\frac{d\lambda_{-i}}{dt} = \eta \lambda_{-i}(t) \cdot R_{-i}(t) - \delta \lambda_{-i}(t)

\tau_{-i}(t) = \tau_{\max} \left( 1 - \exp\left( -\kappa \cdot \mu_{-i}(t) \lambda_{-i}(t) \right) \right)

Substituting $p_a(t, \Sigma_{-i}(t))$ into the security liability equation yields:

\Psi_{\text{security}}(\tau_i) = \sum_{t=1}^{\infty} \gamma^t \left[ \frac{1}{1 + \exp\left( - \frac{\Delta V_{-i}(t, \tau_i, \Sigma_{-i}) - \Theta_{\text{def}}}{\epsilon_{-i} \sigma(\mu_{-i})} \right)} \right] D(t)

This closed loop proves that an unchecked expansion of $S_i$'s horizon $\tau_i$ elevates $\Delta V_{-i}(t)$, driving $p_a(t, \Sigma_{-i}) \to 1$ exponentially unless counterbalanced by defensive deterrence or mutual resource abundance.

7.7 The Security Dilemma

The dynamic coupling formalizes the classic security dilemma: measures taken by one agent to improve its security (increasing $\tau_i$) make the other agent less secure, triggering a counter-response. This is a powerful mechanism for conflict escalation even in abundant environments—unless the environmental abundance is sufficient to make the attack payoff differential negative.

