Chapter 9: Epistemic Horizon Degradation in World Models

9.1 The Problem of Model Error

The theoretical model assumes agents execute accurate rollouts up to their planning horizon. In practice, model-based reinforcement learning architectures suffer from compound prediction errors: errors in state prediction accumulate over rollout horizons, degrading the reliability of distant forecasts.

9.2 Incorporating Model Uncertainty

We incorporate an explicit model uncertainty penalty in the planning objective:

\hat{V}_{\text{expl}} = \mathbb{E}_{\hat{\mathcal{T}}_\phi} \left[ \sum_{t=1}^{H} \gamma^t \left( \hat{R}_t - \beta \cdot \text{Var}_\phi(\hat{s}_t) \right) \right]

where $\text{Var}_\phi(\hat{s}_t)$ represents ensemble variance across world-model heads. This penalty ensures that epistemic uncertainty about distant domains properly discounts the expected value of exploration, preventing overoptimistic expansion based on model error.

9.3 The Effective Horizon

The effective horizon is not merely the nominal planning depth but the depth at which the model's predictive accuracy remains above a minimum threshold:

\tau_{\text{eff}} = \max \left\{ H : \mathbb{E}\left[ \text{Var}_\phi(\hat{s}_H) \right] < \sigma_{\max}^2 \right\}

This formulation connects the theoretical model to practical implementation: agents with high nominal horizon but poor world models may have lower effective horizon than agents with lower nominal horizon but better models.

