Part III: N-Agent Coalition Networks

Chapter 10: Extension to Asymmetric N-Agent Coalition Networks

10.1 Agent Set and Joint Strategy Space

Define the agent set $\mathcal{N} = \{1, 2, \dots, N\}$ where each agent $i \in \mathcal{N}$ possesses an individual cognitive profile $\Sigma_i = (\tau_i, \lambda_i, \epsilon_i, \mu_i)$ and strategy domain $\mathcal{S}_i = [\tau_{\min, i}, \tau_{\max, i}] \subset \mathbb{R}_{++}$. The joint strategy space is the compact convex product space:

\mathcal{S} = \prod_{i=1}^N \mathcal{S}_i \subset \mathbb{R}^N

10.2 Network Architecture and Coalition Structure

Let the interaction structure be governed by a directed, weighted exposure graph $G = (\mathcal{N}, E, \mathbf{A})$, where $\mathbf{A} = [a_{ij}]_{N \times N}$ denotes spatial/strategic vulnerability ($a_{ij} \in [0, 1]$ represents agent $i$'s physical or informational exposure to agent $j$, with $a_{ii} = 0$).

Coalition alliances are parameterized by a symmetric coordination matrix $\mathbf{C} = [c_{ij}]_{N \times N}$, where $c_{ij} \in [0, 1]$ represents coalition alignment ($c_{ij} = 1$ denotes complete non-aggression and mutual defense; $c_{ij} = 0$ denotes total non-alignment).

The effective threat weighting between agent $i$ and agent $j$ is:

\omega_{ij} = a_{ij} (1 - c_{ij})

10.3 Pairwise Endogenous Attack Probability

The probability that agent $j$ executes an attack against agent $i$ is:

p_a^{(i, j)}(\tau_i, \tau_j) = \sigma \left( \frac{\Delta V_{j \to i}(\tau_i, \tau_j) - \Theta_{\text{def}, i}}{K_j} \right)

where $\Delta V_{j \to i}$ is agent $j$'s perceived payoff differential from attacking agent $i$, $\Theta_{\text{def}, i}$ is agent $i$'s defense threshold, and $K_j = \epsilon_j \sqrt{\frac{\sigma_0^2}{1 + \alpha \mu_j}}$.

10.4 Net Utility Functional

The net utility functional for agent $i$ under the joint profile $\boldsymbol{\tau} = (\tau_1, \dots, \tau_N) \in \mathcal{S}$ is:

U_i(\tau_i, \boldsymbol{\tau}_{-i}) = V_i(\tau_i) - C_i(\tau_i) - \frac{1}{1 - \gamma} \sum_{j \neq i} \omega_{ij} p_a^{(i, j)}(\tau_i, \tau_j) D_{ij}

where $D_{ij}$ is the asset damage inflicted on agent $i$ by agent $j$.

10.5 Asymmetric Node-Specific Critical Temporal Horizon

The payoff differential between distant exploration and local domination for agent $i$ in the N-agent network is:

\Delta V_{\text{expl} - \text{dom}, i}(\tau_i, \boldsymbol{\tau}_{-i}) = -C_{\text{travel}, i}(\lambda_i) + (\tau_i - 1) \left[ \Delta R_{\text{flow}, i}(\lambda_i, \epsilon_i, \mathcal{R}_i) - \sum_{j \neq i} \omega_{ij} \left( p_a^{(i, j)} + p_a^{(j, i)} \right) D_{ij} \right] - R_{\text{local}, i} + C_{\text{conflict}, i}(\lambda_i, \mu_i)

Differentiating with respect to planning horizon $\tau_i$ yields the local strategy gradient:

\frac{\partial \Delta V_{\text{expl} - \text{dom}, i}}{\partial \tau_i} = \Delta R_{\text{flow}, i} - \sum_{j \neq i} \omega_{ij} \left( p_a^{(i, j)} + p_a^{(j, i)} \right) D_{ij}

Setting $\Delta V_{\text{expl} - \text{dom}, i} = 0$ yields the node-specific critical horizon $\tau_{c, i}$ for agent $i$:

\tau_{c, i}(\boldsymbol{\tau}_{-i}) = 1 + \frac{R_{\text{local}, i} + C_{\text{travel}, i}(\lambda_i) - C_{\text{conflict}, i}(\lambda_i, \mu_i)}{\Delta R_{\text{flow}, i}(\lambda_i, \epsilon_i, \mathcal{R}_i) - \sum_{j \neq i} \omega_{ij} \left( p_a^{(i, j)} + p_a^{(j, i)} \right) D_{ij}}

10.6 Network Scaling Implications

Proposition 10.1 (Network Exposure Effect). An agent's threshold for transition to exploration is inversely proportional to its unmitigated network exposure. Dense rivalry networks ($\sum_{j \neq i} \omega_{ij} \to \text{large}$) inflate $\tau_{c, i}$, pushing agents toward preemptive local domination. High coalition density ($c_{ij} \to 1 \implies \omega_{ij} \to 0$) depresses the denominator's threat sum, reducing $\tau_{c, i}$ and enabling long-horizon coexistence.

Proposition 10.2 (Coalition Peace Dividend). If all agents in a network form complete coalitions ($c_{ij} = 1$ for all $i \neq j$), then $\omega_{ij} = 0$ for all pairs, and the threat sum vanishes. The critical horizon reduces to the two-agent baseline:

\tau_{c, i} = 1 + \frac{R_{\text{local}, i} + C_{\text{travel}, i}(\lambda_i) - C_{\text{conflict}, i}(\lambda_i, \mu_i)}{\Delta R_{\text{flow}, i}(\lambda_i, \epsilon_i, \mathcal{R}_i)}

Complete trust thus eliminates the security dilemma and enables purely economic decision-making.

