Appendix C: Proof of the Routh–Hurwitz Criterion for the Two-Agent Case

Claim: For a $2 \times 2$ real matrix $J = \begin{bmatrix} -a_{11} & a_{12} \\ a_{21} & -a_{22} \end{bmatrix}$ with $a_{ii} > 0$, all eigenvalues have strictly negative real part if and only if $\operatorname{Tr}(J) < 0$ and $\operatorname{Det}(J) > 0$.

Proof:

The characteristic polynomial is

p(\lambda) = \det(J - \lambda I) = \lambda^2 - \operatorname{Tr}(J) \lambda + \operatorname{Det}(J).

For a $2 \times 2$ matrix, the eigenvalues are

\lambda_{1,2} = \frac{\operatorname{Tr}(J) \pm \sqrt{\operatorname{Tr}(J)^2 - 4 \operatorname{Det}(J)}}{2}.

Case 1: $\operatorname{Tr}(J)^2 - 4 \operatorname{Det}(J) \ge 0$ (real roots).
Since $\operatorname{Det}(J) > 0$, both roots have the same sign (product positive). Since $\operatorname{Tr}(J) < 0$, the sum is negative, so both roots are negative. Indeed, the square root term is nonnegative and less than $|\operatorname{Tr}(J)|$ because $\operatorname{Tr}(J)^2 - 4 \operatorname{Det}(J) < \operatorname{Tr}(J)^2$ (since $\operatorname{Det}(J) > 0$). Hence both roots are negative.

Case 2: $\operatorname{Tr}(J)^2 - 4 \operatorname{Det}(J) < 0$ (complex conjugate roots).
The real part is $\operatorname{Tr}(J)/2 < 0$, so $\operatorname{Re}(\lambda_{1,2}) < 0$.

In both cases, all eigenvalues lie in the open left half-plane. The converse follows from the fact that if any eigenvalue has nonnegative real part, then either the trace is nonnegative (if real) or the determinant is nonpositive (if product of eigenvalues is nonpositive). $\blacksquare$

