Appendix D: Proof of Berge’s Maximum Theorem for the Best-Response Correspondence

Claim: Let $U_i: \mathcal{S}_i \times \mathcal{S}_{-i} \to \mathbb{R}$ be continuous and strictly concave in $\tau_i$ for each fixed $\tau_{-i}$, with $\mathcal{S}_i$ compact and convex. Then the best-response correspondence $\mathcal{B}_i(\tau_{-i}) = \arg\max_{\tau_i \in \mathcal{S}_i} U_i(\tau_i, \tau_{-i})$ is single-valued and continuous.

Proof:

Single-valuedness: Strict concavity of $U_i$ in $\tau_i$ on the convex set $\mathcal{S}_i$ implies that the maximizer is unique. (If two distinct points $\tau_i^1 \neq \tau_i^2$ both maximize, then for any $\theta \in (0,1)$, strict concavity gives $U_i(\theta \tau_i^1 + (1-\theta)\tau_i^2) > \theta U_i(\tau_i^1) + (1-\theta) U_i(\tau_i^2) = \max U_i$, a contradiction.)

Continuity: By Berge’s Maximum Theorem, if $U_i$ is continuous on $\mathcal{S}_i \times \mathcal{S}_{-i}$ and the feasible correspondence is constant (hence continuous), then the argmax correspondence is upper hemicontinuous. Since the correspondence is single-valued, upper hemicontinuity coincides with continuity of the function $\mathcal{B}_i$. $\blacksquare$

