Appendix E: Proof of Brouwer’s Fixed Point Theorem (Sketch)

Claim: Let $\mathcal{S} \subset \mathbb{R}^N$ be non-empty, compact, and convex, and let $f: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{S}$ be continuous. Then $f$ has at least one fixed point.

Proof sketch:

For $N=1$, this is the Intermediate Value Theorem: any continuous $f: [a,b] \to [a,b]$ must intersect the diagonal.

For $N \ge 2$, the standard proof proceeds by contradiction using the No-Retraction Theorem: there is no continuous map from the closed unit ball $B^N$ to its boundary $S^{N-1}$ that fixes every point on the boundary. If $f$ had no fixed point, one could construct such a retraction by projecting from $f(x)$ through $x$ to the boundary, contradicting the theorem. The No-Retraction Theorem itself is proved via algebraic topology (e.g., using the fact that $S^{N-1}$ is not a retract of $B^N$). Details are standard in topology textbooks. $\blacksquare$

