Appendix F: Proof of Gershgorin’s Circle Theorem

Claim: Let $A = [a_{ij}]$ be an $N \times N$ complex matrix. Then every eigenvalue $\lambda$ of $A$ lies in at least one Gershgorin disc

D_i = \left\{ z \in \mathbb{C} : |z - a_{ii}| \le \sum_{j \neq i} |a_{ij}| \right\}.

Proof:

Let $\lambda$ be an eigenvalue of $A$ with eigenvector $\mathbf{x} = (x_1, \dots, x_N)^T \neq 0$. Choose index $i$ such that $|x_i| = \max_j |x_j| > 0$. From $A\mathbf{x} = \lambda \mathbf{x}$, the $i$-th row gives

(\lambda - a_{ii}) x_i = \sum_{j \neq i} a_{ij} x_j.

Taking absolute values and applying the triangle inequality:

|\lambda - a_{ii}| \, |x_i| = \left| \sum_{j \neq i} a_{ij} x_j \right| \le \sum_{j \neq i} |a_{ij}| \, |x_j| \le |x_i| \sum_{j \neq i} |a_{ij}|.

Dividing by $|x_i| > 0$ yields the desired inequality. $\blacksquare$

