Appendix G: Detailed Derivation of Payoff Functions and Security Liability

This appendix provides the step-by-step construction of the three strategic payoffs and the dynamic security liability term.

G.1 Dynamic Security Liability

Consider agent $S_i$ that leaves a local adversary $S_{-i}$ unneutralized. In each discrete period $t = 1, 2, \dots$, the adversary attacks with probability $p_a$, causing damage $D$. The present value of expected losses is

\Omega_{\text{threat}} = \sum_{t=1}^{\infty} \gamma^t p_a D = p_a D \frac{\gamma}{1-\gamma} = p_a D (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1),

where we define the effective horizon $\tau_{\text{eff}} = 1/(1-\gamma)$ so that $\gamma/(1-\gamma) = \tau_{\text{eff}} - 1$. The security premium of neutralizing the adversary (i.e., the avoided loss) is exactly $\Psi_{\text{security}} = (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1) p_a D$.

G.2 Strategy Payoffs

Local Domination $A_{\text{dom}}$:
The agent pays a one-time conflict cost $C_{\text{conflict}}(\lambda, \mu)$, gains an immediate resource stock $R_{\text{local}}$, and thereafter enjoys a per-period flow $R_{\text{local, flow}}$ without threat. The present value is

V(A_{\text{dom}}) = R_{\text{local}} - C_{\text{conflict}} + \sum_{t=1}^{\infty} \gamma^t R_{\text{local, flow}} = R_{\text{local}} - C_{\text{conflict}} + (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1) R_{\text{local, flow}}.

Because the adversary is neutralized, the avoided loss $p_a D$ is implicitly added to the flow: the net per-period gain relative to a baseline with threat is $R_{\text{local, flow}} + p_a D$. Thus we can write

V(A_{\text{dom}}) = R_{\text{local}} - C_{\text{conflict}} + (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1)(R_{\text{local, flow}} + p_a D).

Distant Exploration $A_{\text{expl}}$:
The agent pays a one-time travel cost $C_{\text{travel}}(\lambda)$, then receives a per-period expected distant yield $\mathbb{E}[R_{\text{distant}}]$ while still facing the local threat (probability $p_a$ each period). The present value is

V(A_{\text{expl}}) = -C_{\text{travel}} + \sum_{t=1}^{\infty} \gamma^t \left( \mathbb{E}[R_{\text{distant}}] - p_a D \right) = -C_{\text{travel}} + (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1)(\mathbb{E}[R_{\text{distant}}] - p_a D).

Cooperative Alignment $A_{\text{coop}}$:
The agent pays a one-time diplomacy cost $C_{\text{diplomacy}}(\mu)$, then receives a per-period shared yield $R_{\text{shared}}(\mathcal{S})$ with a reduced attack probability $\delta(\mu) p_a$, where $\delta(\mu) \in [0,1)$ and $\delta'(\mu) < 0$. The present value is

V(A_{\text{coop}}) = -C_{\text{diplomacy}} + (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1)\left( R_{\text{shared}} - \delta(\mu) p_a D \right).

G.3 Assumptions on Cost Functions

· $C_{\text{conflict}}(\lambda, \mu)$: increasing in $\lambda$ (larger reach means more expensive conflict) and decreasing in $\mu$ (better modeling reduces conflict cost).
· $C_{\text{travel}}(\lambda)$: increasing in $\lambda$ (longer travel is costlier).
· $C_{\text{diplomacy}}(\mu)$: decreasing in $\mu$ (better modeling reduces negotiation cost).
· $\mathbb{E}[R_{\text{distant}}]$ depends on $\lambda$ (reach), $\epsilon$ (exploration propensity), and $\mathcal{R}$ (abundance). It is increasing in all three.
· $R_{\text{shared}}$ depends on $\mathcal{S}$ and is increasing.
· $R_{\text{local, flow}}$ may depend on $\mathcal{R}$ and local resource stock.

These dependencies are used qualitatively in the main text.

