Appendix H: Derivation of the Critical Temporal Horizon and Sign Reversal Theorem

H.1 Payoff Differential

Define

\Delta V_{\text{expl} - \text{dom}} = V(A_{\text{expl}}) - V(A_{\text{dom}}).

Substituting the expressions from Appendix G:

\begin{aligned}
\Delta V_{\text{expl} - \text{dom}} &= \left[ -C_{\text{travel}} + (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1)(\mathbb{E}[R_{\text{distant}}] - p_a D) \right] \\
&\quad - \left[ R_{\text{local}} - C_{\text{conflict}} + (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1)(R_{\text{local, flow}} + p_a D) \right] \\
&= -C_{\text{travel}} - R_{\text{local}} + C_{\text{conflict}} + (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1)\left( \mathbb{E}[R_{\text{distant}}] - R_{\text{local, flow}} - 2 p_a D \right).
\end{aligned}

Introduce $\Delta R_{\text{flow}} := \mathbb{E}[R_{\text{distant}}] - R_{\text{local, flow}}$. Then

\Delta V_{\text{expl} - \text{dom}} = -C_{\text{travel}} - R_{\text{local}} + C_{\text{conflict}} + (\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1)(\Delta R_{\text{flow}} - 2 p_a D).

H.2 The Governing Derivative

Differentiating with respect to $\tau_{\text{eff}}$ (treating $\tau_{\text{eff}}$ as a continuous variable via $\gamma$):

\frac{\partial}{\partial \tau_{\text{eff}}} \Delta V_{\text{expl} - \text{dom}} = \Delta R_{\text{flow}} - 2 p_a D.

This is the central derivative in the Horizon-Conflict Sign Reversal Theorem.

H.3 Critical Horizon

Exploration strictly dominates domination when $\Delta V_{\text{expl} - \text{dom}} > 0$. Solving for $\tau_{\text{eff}}$:

(\tau_{\text{eff}} - 1)(\Delta R_{\text{flow}} - 2 p_a D) > R_{\text{local}} + C_{\text{travel}} - C_{\text{conflict}}.

If $\Delta R_{\text{flow}} - 2 p_a D > 0$, then

\tau_{\text{eff}} > 1 + \frac{R_{\text{local}} + C_{\text{travel}} - C_{\text{conflict}}}{\Delta R_{\text{flow}} - 2 p_a D} =: \tau_c.

If $\Delta R_{\text{flow}} - 2 p_a D < 0$, the inequality reverses: exploration dominates for horizons below the (possibly negative) threshold, but for large horizons domination dominates. Since the denominator is negative, the right-hand side may be less than 1, meaning no finite positive horizon satisfies exploration dominance; domination remains optimal for all $\tau_{\text{eff}} \ge 1$. Thus $\tau_c$ is undefined or infinite in the scarce regime.

H.4 Monotonicity and Propensity

Let $C_{\text{dom}}$ denote the equilibrium proportion of actions allocated to domination. In a deterministic best-response setting, $C_{\text{dom}}$ is monotone decreasing in $\Delta V_{\text{expl} - \text{dom}}$ (as exploration becomes relatively more attractive, less domination is chosen). Therefore $\partial C_{\text{dom}}/\partial \tau_{\text{eff}}$ has the opposite sign of $\partial \Delta V/\partial \tau_{\text{eff}}$, yielding the sign reversal result. $\blacksquare$

