Appendix I: Endogenous Attack Probability: Derivation and Properties

I.1 Model Setup

Adversary $S_{-i}$ at time $t$ estimates the net benefit of attacking agent $S_i$ versus coexisting:

\Delta V_{-i}(t) = \mathbb{E}_{\mu_{-i}} \left[ \sum_{k=1}^{\tau_{-i}(t)} \gamma^k \left( \hat{R}_{-i}^{\text{attack}}(t+k) - \hat{R}_{-i}^{\text{coexist}}(t+k) \right) \right] - C_{\text{attack}}(\lambda_{-i}(t)).

The estimate is noisy: the adversary's information is limited, and the variance of the estimation error decreases with modeling capacity:

\sigma^2(\mu_{-i}) = \frac{\sigma_0^2}{1 + \alpha \mu_{-i}}.

Assume the adversary's decision follows a random utility model with logistic noise. The probability of attack is

p_a(t, \Sigma_{-i}) = \Pr\left( \Delta V_{-i}(t) + \xi > \Theta_{\text{def}} \right),

where $\xi$ is a logistic random variable with scale $K_{-i} = \epsilon_{-i} \sigma(\mu_{-i})$ and $\epsilon_{-i}$ is the risk tolerance. The logistic CDF gives

p_a(t, \Sigma_{-i}) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp\left( -\frac{\Delta V_{-i}(t) - \Theta_{\text{def}}}{K_{-i}} \right)}.

I.2 Limiting Behavior

· As $\mu_{-i} \to \infty$ (perfect model) and $\epsilon_{-i} \to 0$ (no noise), $K_{-i} \to 0$. The logistic function approaches a step: $p_a \to 1$ if $\Delta V_{-i} > \Theta_{\text{def}}$, else $0$.
· As $\epsilon_{-i} \to \infty$ (extreme risk tolerance or randomness), $K_{-i} \to \infty$, the argument $\to 0$, so $p_a \to 1/2$.
· As $\Delta V_{-i} \to \pm\infty$, $p_a \to 1$ or $0$, respectively.

I.3 Dynamic Coupling

The cognitive profile of the adversary evolves. We model spatial reach growth as

\frac{d\lambda_{-i}}{dt} = \eta \lambda_{-i}(t) R_{-i}(t) - \delta \lambda_{-i}(t),

where $R_{-i}(t)$ is resource intake and $\delta$ is decay. Temporal horizon is linked to modeling capacity and reach via

\tau_{-i}(t) = \tau_{\max} \left( 1 - \exp\left( -\kappa \mu_{-i}(t) \lambda_{-i}(t) \right) \right),

capturing that planning depth grows with the ability to act over larger scales. Substituting $p_a(t, \Sigma_{-i}(t))$ into the security liability yields a time-varying threat term, which in the main text is represented as

\Psi_{\text{security}}(\tau_i) = \sum_{t=1}^{\infty} \gamma^t p_a(t, \Sigma_{-i}(t)) D(t).

The feedback loop: an increase in $\tau_i$ (defender's planning horizon) may raise $\Delta V_{-i}$ if the defender's extended horizon is perceived as a greater threat, increasing $p_a$ and thus the security liability.

