Appendix L: Additional Mathematical Background

L.1 Strict Concavity and Uniqueness of Maximizer

A function $f: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}$ on a convex set is strictly concave if for all $x \neq y$ and $\theta \in (0,1)$,

f(\theta x + (1-\theta)y) > \theta f(x) + (1-\theta) f(y).

If $f$ is strictly concave on a convex set, it has at most one maximizer. Proof: if two distinct maximizers existed, the strict inequality would contradict optimality.

L.2 Negative Definiteness and Lyapunov Stability

A symmetric matrix $M$ is negative definite if $\mathbf{x}^T M \mathbf{x} < 0$ for all $\mathbf{x} \neq 0$. For the linear system $\dot{\mathbf{x}} = A\mathbf{x}$, if the symmetric part $(A+A^T)/2$ is negative definite, then the origin is asymptotically stable. This follows from the Lyapunov function $V(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{x}^T \mathbf{x}$: $\dot{V} = \mathbf{x}^T (A+A^T)\mathbf{x} = 2\mathbf{x}^T M \mathbf{x} < 0$.

L.3 Rosen's Diagonally Strict Concavity

For an N-player game with payoff functions $U_i(\mathbf{x})$, the pseudogradient is $g(\mathbf{x}) = (\nabla_{x_1} U_1, \dots, \nabla_{x_N} U_N)^T$. The game is diagonally strictly concave if there exists a fixed vector $r > 0$ such that for all $\mathbf{x} \neq \mathbf{y}$,

(\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{y})^T [r \circ g(\mathbf{x}) - r \circ g(\mathbf{y})] > 0.

This condition ensures uniqueness of Nash equilibrium and convergence of gradient dynamics. In our setting, the condition reduces to the diagonal dominance of the Jacobian's symmetric part.

L.4 Gershgorin Discs and Diagonal Dominance

A matrix is strictly diagonally dominant if $|a_{ii}| > \sum_{j \neq i} |a_{ij}|$ for all $i$. By Gershgorin's theorem, such a matrix with negative diagonal entries is negative definite (all eigenvalues in the left half-plane).

