Appendix N: Additional Theorems and Lemmas

N.1 Lemma: Monotonicity of $C_{\text{dom}}$ in $\Delta V$

Assume that the probability of choosing domination is a smooth decreasing function of the payoff difference $\Delta V = V(A_{\text{expl}}) - V(A_{\text{dom}})$, i.e., $C_{\text{dom}} = f(\Delta V)$ with $f' < 0$. Then $\frac{\partial C_{\text{dom}}}{\partial \tau} = f'(\Delta V) \cdot \frac{\partial \Delta V}{\partial \tau}$. Since $f' < 0$, the sign of $\partial C_{\text{dom}}/\partial \tau$ is opposite to the sign of $\partial \Delta V/\partial \tau$. This justifies the sign-reversal interpretation.

N.2 Lemma: Boundedness of Logistic Derivatives

The logistic function $\sigma(z) = 1/(1+e^{-z})$ has derivatives bounded by $\sup_z |\sigma'(z)| = 1/4$ and $\sup_z |\sigma''(z)| \le 1/6\sqrt{3} \approx 0.096$. This ensures that the Jacobian entries are bounded, supporting the existence of maxima in Assumption J.2.

N.3 Corollary: Existence of Critical Horizon under Abundance

If $\Delta R_{\text{flow}} > 2p_a D$ and the numerator $R_{\text{local}} + C_{\text{travel}} - C_{\text{conflict}} > 0$, then $\tau_c > 1$ is finite, and for all $\tau_{\text{eff}} > \tau_c$, exploration strictly dominates domination. If the numerator is negative, then exploration dominates for all horizons, so $\tau_c$ can be set to 1.

